      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Mumps disease",
      "pageStart": 11,
      "pageEnd": 12,
      "target": "../sections/sec-004-11-mumps-disease.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Mumps disease  \nMumps is an acute viral illness characterized by unilateral or bilateral tenderness \nor swelling of the parotid or other salivary glands. Mumps is transmitted through \nperson-to-person contact or by direct contact with respiratory droplets or saliva from \nan infected person. By comparison to measles and varicella, which can be transmitted \nby aerosol spread, mumps is less infectious (Hope-Simpson, 1952). The mumps virus \nreplicates in the nasopharynx and regional lymph nodes, with a secondary viremia \noccurring late in the incubation period. During those three to five days of v",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes mumps as an acute viral illness marked by swelling of the parotid or other salivary glands, spread by contact with respiratory droplets or saliva and less infectious than measles or varicella. It explains how the virus replicates and spreads in the body and the timing of infectiousness. It lists complications such as orchitis, meningitis, encephalitis and deafness, with their frequencies.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
