    {
      "id": "library/10665-97885/blocks/prose-sec-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Mumps vaccines",
      "pageStart": 12,
      "pageEnd": 17,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-13-mumps-vaccines.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Mumps vaccines  \nThe first mumps vaccine was developed in 1946 (Habel, 1946). It was based on formalin-\ninactivated virus, but was discontinued because immunity was short-lived (Habel, 1951). \nSince then numerous mumps vaccine strains have been developed and used in vaccines \nthroughout the world. These vaccines have varied efficacy and safety profiles.\n3\n1.3.1\t Jeryl Lynn \nThe first live attenuated mumps vaccine using the Jeryl Lynn strain was developed in the \nUnited States of America (USA) using an isolate from a child with mumps, and passaged \nin embryonated hens’ eggs and chick embryo cel",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reviews mumps vaccines from the first inactivated vaccine of 1946, discontinued because immunity was short-lived, to the many live attenuated strains used since. It describes strains such as Jeryl Lynn, Urabe, Leningrad-3 and others, including how they were developed. A table compares reported rates of aseptic meningitis after vaccination with different strains in various countries.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-97885/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Immunological response to natural infection",
      "pageStart": 17,
      "pageEnd": 17,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-2-immunological-response-to-natural-infection.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The immune response to mumps virus infection is likely to be the result of a complex \ninterplay between both the humoral and cellular arms of the immune response, \nand no definitive correlates of protection have yet been identified. Studies on the immune \nresponse to mumps infection are quite limited in comparison to those described for \nmeasles and rubella infections.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes that immunity after natural mumps infection probably involves both antibodies and cellular immunity, that no definitive correlates of protection exist, and that research is limited compared with measles and rubella.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
