    {
      "id": "library/10665-97885/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Antibody response to mumps infection in unvaccinated individuals",
      "pageStart": 17,
      "pageEnd": 17,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-21-antibody-response-to-mumps-infection-in-unvac.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Antibody response to mumps infection in unvaccinated individuals\nIn naive individuals immunoglobulin M (IgM) antibodies are measurable within a \nfew days of symptom onset. IgM peaks about one week after the onset of parotitis or \nsymptoms, and is detectable for weeks to months after parotitis onset (Ukkonen et al., \n1981; Benito et al., 1987). Low avidity IgG may also be present at the time of symptom \nonset, although generally at a low level (Narita et al., 1998). IgG antibody increases \nrapidly and reaches maximum levels about three weeks after onset of symptoms. \nIgG antibodies remain at th",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the antibody response to mumps in unvaccinated people, with IgM appearing within days of symptoms and peaking around one week. IgG rises to a peak in about three weeks and persists, while salivary IgA can be detected for several weeks.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-97885/blocks/prose-sec-009",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Cell-mediated immunity following natural mumps disease",
      "pageStart": 17,
      "pageEnd": 18,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-22-cell-mediated-immunity-following-natural-mump.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Cell-mediated immunity following natural mumps disease\nLymphocytes are known to play an important role in host response to viral infections, \nand are believed to have a significant function in the immune response to mumps and \nrecovery from mumps infection. While the presence of a plaque reduction neutralization \ntitre to mumps appears to be associated with the development of mumps immunity, \nless is known about the development, significance, and function of mumps-specific \ncell-mediated immunity (CMI). Specific lymphocyte mediated cytotoxicity has been \nshown to correlate well with the presen",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes cell-mediated immunity after natural mumps, noting that lymphocytes are thought to aid recovery but that little is known about mumps-specific cellular immunity. It cites studies linking cytotoxic and proliferative lymphocyte responses with infection.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-97885/blocks/prose-sec-010",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Maternal antibody",
      "pageStart": 18,
      "pageEnd": 18,
      "target": "../sections/sec-010-23-maternal-antibody.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Maternal antibody\nMaternal antibody (IgG) to mumps following natural infection is transferred across \nthe placenta and is believed to provide protection to infants against clinical mumps. \nClinical mumps occurs less frequently in infants aged less than one year (12% to 17%) \ncompared to children aged one to four years (68%) (Philip et al., 1959; Meyer, 1962; \nReed et al., 1967). Also, during an outbreak, two out of three infants under 12 months \nof age born to mothers with no history of clinical mumps developed mumps after \nexposure, while no infants under 12 months of age born to 10 mothers w",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that maternal antibodies to mumps cross the placenta and likely protect infants, citing lower mumps rates in infants under one year. It reports studies on how long maternal antibodies remain detectable during the first year.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-97885/blocks/prose-sec-011",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Duration of immunity to natural mumps infection",
      "pageStart": 18,
      "pageEnd": 19,
      "target": "../sections/sec-011-24-duration-of-immunity-to-natural-mumps-infecti.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Duration of immunity to natural mumps infection\nNatural mumps virus infection is generally believed to provide long-lasting immunity. \nTwenty or more years after their mumps illness, most (82%) individuals still had \ndetectable haemagglutination-inhibiting antibodies (Levitt et al., 1970). However, \ncases of clinically apparent mumps reinfection that have been confirmed with \nepidemiological links or laboratory tests have been reported (Meyer, 1962; Gut et al., \n1995; Crowley & Afzal, 2002; Yoshida et al., 2008), and may be more common than \npreviously thought.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that natural mumps infection is generally thought to give long-lasting immunity, though confirmed reinfections have been reported and may be more common than believed.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
