    {
      "id": "library/10665-97885/blocks/prose-sec-013",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Antibody response following vaccination",
      "pageStart": 19,
      "pageEnd": 21,
      "target": "../sections/sec-013-31-antibody-response-following-vaccination.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Antibody response following vaccination\nIn general, over 90% of infants and children develop detectable antibodies against \nmumps following vaccination with mumps vaccines (Table 2). Seroconversion rates \nare comparable for vaccine combinations with Jeryl Lynn, RIT 4385 and Urabe Am \n9 strains (Isomura et al., 1973; Vesikari et al., 1983b; Usonis et al., 1998; Usonis et \nal., 1999; Usonis et al., 2001; Lee et al., 2002) except for one study that suggested \nhigher serconversion for children receiving vaccines containing Urabe Am 9 than \nthose receiving Jeryl Lynn-containing vaccines (Vesikari e",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reports that over 90% of children develop mumps antibodies after vaccination, with similar seroconversion across major vaccine strains, and discusses responses to one and two doses. A table summarizes seroconversion studies. It notes that revaccination raised antibody titres in more than half of recipients and reduced the share with low titres.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
