    {
      "id": "library/10665-97885/blocks/prose-sec-014",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Cell-mediated immunity following mumps vaccination",
      "pageStart": 21,
      "pageEnd": 21,
      "target": "../sections/sec-014-33-cell-mediated-immunity-following-mumps-vaccin.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Cell-mediated immunity following mumps vaccination\nFollowing vaccination with live attenuated mumps vaccine, most, but not all children \nwith anti-mumps antibody in their sera, demonstrated a lymphocyte proliferative \nresponse to mumps antigen (Ilonen, 1979; Ilonen et al., 1984). Unlike the humoral \nimmune response to mumps, cellular responses were equivalent in all age groups and \nwere independent of the presence of maternal antibody (Gans et al., 2001). In addition, \nassociations of specific histocompatibility leukocyte antigen (HLA) haplotypes \nwith higher or lower frequencies of mumps anti",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reports that most vaccinated children with mumps antibodies also show lymphocyte responses, that cellular responses are similar across ages regardless of maternal antibody, and that HLA associations suggest genetic influence on the immune response.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-97885/blocks/prose-sec-015",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Duration of immunity to mumps vaccination",
      "pageStart": 21,
      "pageEnd": 22,
      "target": "../sections/sec-015-34-duration-of-immunity-to-mumps-vaccination.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Duration of immunity to mumps vaccination \nData regarding long-term immunity against mumps after vaccination are limited. \nStudies indicate that one dose of MMR vaccine can provide persistent antibodies \nto mumps. Between 70% and 99% of individuals had detectable anti-mumps \nantibodies using enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) or neutralization tests \napproximately ten years after initial vaccination (Broliden et al., 1998; Gothefors et al., \n2001; LeBaron et al., 2009) (Table 3). Differences in laboratory method may account for \nthe wide variation in detection rates. In addition, among",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes that data on long-term immunity after mumps vaccination are limited, though antibodies persist in most people about ten years after one MMR dose, with variation by laboratory method. It discusses waning antibody levels after two doses. It ends by listing open questions on cellular immunity and boosting from exposure to wild virus.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-97885/blocks/prose-sec-016",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Correlates of immunity",
      "pageStart": 22,
      "pageEnd": 24,
      "target": "../sections/sec-016-35-correlates-of-immunity.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Correlates of immunity\nHumoral immunity is important in protection against mumps, and antibody \nmeasurements are often used as a surrogate measure of immunity to viral infections. \nHowever, there is poor correlation between assays that measure neutralization and less \nlabour-intensive methods that measure the presence of mumps antibody (Pipkin et al., \n1999). While no serological test available for mumps consistently and reliably predicts \nimmunity, neutralizing antibodies appear to be a reasonable marker for immunity. \nAntibodies directed against haemagglutinin-neuraminidase protein (HN) have",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Discusses correlates of immunity to mumps, explaining that neutralizing antibodies seem a reasonable marker though no serological test reliably predicts protection. It calls for more research, including on cell-mediated immunity markers.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-97885/blocks/prose-sec-017",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Laboratory diagnosis of mumps",
      "pageStart": 24,
      "pageEnd": 24,
      "target": "../sections/sec-017-4-laboratory-diagnosis-of-mumps.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "14\nA clinical diagnosis of mumps is frequently made when parotitis is evident at the time \nof patient examination. However, since parotitis may be caused by other viral or non-\nviral diseases or conditions, laboratory confirmation using virological or serological \ntechniques may be needed, especially as mumps disease becomes rare due to increased \nvaccination.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes that mumps is often diagnosed clinically from parotitis but that laboratory confirmation may be needed because other causes exist and mumps is becoming rare.",
