      "target": "../sections/sec-019-42-serological-methods.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Serological methods\nDetection of mumps-specific IgM antibody in serum or saliva is a good diagnostic \nmeasure in unvaccinated patients. Timing of specimen collection is important to \nconsider in interpreting laboratory results. Negative IgM ELISA results may occur \nwhen serum is collected prior to day four of clinical presentation (Cunningham et al., \n2006; Krause et al., 2007). By contrast, patients who mount a secondary immune \nresponse, as occurs in the majority of vaccinated mumps cases, may not have an \nIgM response, or it may be transient and not detected depending on timing of specimen",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes serological diagnosis of mumps, explaining that IgM detection works well in unvaccinated patients if specimens are well timed but may fail in vaccinated people with a secondary immune response. A table sets out expected laboratory results by vaccination status and timing of collection.",
