      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Vaccine effectiveness\n5.2.1\t Vaccine effectiveness of one dose\nIn postlicensure studies, vaccine effectiveness estimates for prevention of mumps \ndisease have been lower (Table 5). In 18 studies from outbreaks (primarily school\nchildren) in North America and Europe, the median estimate for vaccine effectiveness \nof one dose of the Jeryl Lynn mumps vaccine was 79% (range: 62% to 91%) \n(Lewis et al., 1979; Kim-Farley et al., 1985; Sullivan et al., 1985; Chaiken et al., 1987; \nWharton et al., 1988; Hersh et al., 1991; Cheek et al., 1995; Toscani et al., 1996; \nChamot et al., 1998; Schlegel et al.",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reviews postlicensure vaccine effectiveness from outbreak studies, with a median of 79% for one dose of Jeryl Lynn vaccine and lower figures for the Urabe strain. It reports that the Rubini strain showed little or no effectiveness and is no longer available. Two-dose Jeryl Lynn effectiveness had a median of 88%, usually but not significantly higher than one dose. It calls for research linking protection to neutralizing antibody levels and on the role of heterologous mumps strains.",
